 ],
 "literal": "Mountain intervening placed country-man-also he-is-not / Tree tops not-appearing town-man-also he-is-not / With-eyes to-see near place even-though-being / Deity approached devotees like / Avoiding who-behaves to-my-lord / I-do-not-grieve indeed I once one time-indeed",
 "translation": "He is not a man from a country separated by intervening mountains. He is not a man from a town so distant that its treetops cannot be seen. Even though he is in a place close enough to be seen with the eyes, he avoids me and behaves like devotees who keep a respectful distance from a deity. For my lord who conducts himself thus, I did not grieve once upon a time, but now I do.",
 "modern": "அவர் மலைகளால் பிரிக்கப்பட்ட நாட்டைச் சேர்ந்தவரும் அல்லர். மரங்களின் உச்சிகள் கூடத் தெரியாத தூரத்து ஊரைச் சேர்ந்தவரும் அல்லர். கண்ணால் பார்க்கக்கூடிய மிக அருகில் இருந்தும், தெய்வத்தை நெருங்கும் பூசாரிகளைப் போல என்னை விலகி நடக்கும் என் தலைவனுக்காக, நான் முன்பு ஒரு காலத்தில் வருந்தாமல் இருந்தேன்.",
 "explanation": "In this Marutam poem, the heroine expresses her deep sorrow to her confidante regarding the hero's emotional distance. She points out that he does not live in a far-off land separated by mountains, nor in a distant town whose treetops are invisible. Although he is close enough to be seen, he deliberately avoids her, behaving like a cautious devotee who maintains a respectful distance from a powerful deity. This striking comparison highlights how the hero treats his own wife with ritualistic avoidance rather than intimate affection. The heroine laments that while she did not grieve over his behavior in the past, his continued indifference now causes her immense pain. The setting of the same town emphasizes the irony of physical proximity paired with emotional alienation, which is central to the Marutam landscape of infidelity and sulking.",
